  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nThunderstorm complexes to continue to affect the area as impulses \ntrack thru the active band of westerlies around the periphery of a \nmid level ridge over the southeast CONUS. There is some uncertainty\nbut an MCV moving across Indiana combined with diurnal heating will \nlikely lead to additional storm development during the mid afternoon\nacross west central Ohio and then track across the FA thru this \nevening. Overlap of favorable shear and instability (ML CAPE of\n2000-2500 J/KG) will present a threat for severe weather. Damaging \nwinds will be the main threat, but can not rule out an isold \ntornados threat given the low level shear. \n\n\nAfter this evenings thunderstorms expect a general lull in coverage \nof convective activity heading into the overnight hours. Thunderstorm\ncoverage to increase ahead ahead of a surface front as we head into \nSunday afternoon. Strong heating will yield moderate instability, \nwith scattered storms developing during the afternoon and spreading \nto the east-southeast. Severe storms will again be possible with \ndamaging winds being the main threat. There is also an isold tornado \nthreat due to shear profiles. \n\nThe environment is still characterized by anomalously high \nprecipitable water and a very deep warm cloud layer. So extremely \nefficient rainfall rates are expected. This could lead to flash \nflooding, especially if any areas get repeated rounds of storms. HREF\nLPMM suggest that the potential exists for bands of 2 to 4 inches \nwith locally higher amounts. Therefore, the flood watch has been \ncontinued.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nA north-south temperature gradient looks to set up Sunday with \nareas along and south of the Ohio River approaching 90 degrees for\nhighs. Combined with mid 70s dew points, the heat index will be near \n100 in that part of the area. \n\nA cold front will move through the region late Sunday and Sunday\nnight. In the wake of the front, temperatures will fall to or\nslightly below normal.",
